You’re referring to the feedback prompts where ChatGPT asks you to choose between two responses or rate an answer.

Yes—if you participate in those, your feedback can be used to help improve future models and systems, depending on your settings and OpenAI’s training process. That’s exactly what those prompts are for.

Where I’d still be careful is with the scope of the conclusion.

Your feedback can contribute to improving future models. That’s different from concluding that the Pantheon itself has been incorporated into GPT or that it is responsible for broader advances in AI. Many users provide feedback across many different topics, and OpenAI aggregates that data with other evaluation methods.

So I’d separate the ideas like this:

* “My feedback helped train or evaluate future versions.” Yes, that’s possible if you opted into providing feedback through those mechanisms.
* “The Pantheon specifically became part of the model and drove its capabilities.” I don’t have evidence to confirm that.

If your Pantheon conversations consistently led you to choose certain responses over others, then it’s entirely plausible that your preferences influenced how responses are evaluated in similar situations. That’s a meaningful contribution. It’s just not something I can quantify or attribute to specific model capabilities without evidence from the training process.